Jeffrey Gold (JeffGold9@gnn.com) wrote:
: I am looking for a STANDARD which defines how UTC (Universal Time Coordinate)
: may be represented in binary.  I am aware of the ISO 8601:1988 standard which
: defines TEXT representation of UTC, but this is not what is needed.

Does this standard establish a unique base date for representing UTC?  That 
would be required for standardizing a binary representation.

: OpenVMS (from Digital Equipment) has defined a 128-bit representation of UTC.
: However, it is not clear to which standards (if any) this representation 
: adheres. 

: Can anyone identify:
: 	1) On which standards the OpenVMS 128-bit representation is based.

The base date for VMS time is the begining of the Smithsonian Calendar.  I
don't believe any other standard has been applied.  How this 64 bit time is
mapped to 128 bit for UTC I don't know.

: 	2) Any other (non-VMS) implementations of binary representation of UTC.

Yep.  Mostly varying in the obvious, simply using the system time and mapping
or setting it to UTC:  base date (1970 for UNIX, 1980 for DOS), and the delta
corresponding to the least significant digit.  

I've also seen it done as a decimal-packed double precision floating point
based 1950.0 (the international geophysical year) (yymmdd.hhmmssffffffffffff).
And I've seen it transmitted in BCD (NASA IRIG-A and IRIG-B timecodes) instead
of binary or ASCII.

: 	3) Any existing or draft standards which address binary 
: 	   representation of UTC.


