The UTC timestamp is intentionally opaque so that its interpretation can
be changed.
I don't agree with this position, but understand the sentiment.  This was
defined as
part of DECdts which was proposed and accepted as part of the OSF DCE
technology
and the concern was that the OSF process would require changes to the
binary format.

This format is incorporated in DECnet OSI for both OpenVMS and Digital
UNIX as
part of DECdts support.  And the format is also used by DEC's DCE product
offerings
for which the DCE client license is included with the OS, but not
necessarily the kit.
I'm not sure if the DCE binary format is specified for interoperability
between hardware
architectures (big vs little endien).

So much for the history.

The UTC time stamp has 4 components.  The first is a 64 bit timestamp that
is
the 100ns units since the date that the Gregorian calendar was adjusted. 
The second
is 48 bits of inaccuracy in units of 100 ns.  The third field is 12 bits
of TDF in minute
units.  And the fourth field is the version in 4 bits.

